When a browser or the frontend calls the backend, every request carries a Host header naming the server it was addressed to. Your frontend sends requests to https://django-no-redis-74168f6e866e.herokuapp.com/..., so each request arrives with Host: django-no-redis-74168f6e866e.herokuapp.com.\
Django compares that header with ALLOWED_HOSTS on every request. If it doesn't match an entry, Django refuses the request with 400 Bad Request and never runs the view. Remove that hostname and the whole backend stops working: the API, logins, the admin, everything.\
Why Django checks it: an attacker can send a request with a fake Host header. Django uses the host to build full URLs, such as links in password-reset emails. Without the check, an attacker could request a reset for a student's account with Host: evil.com, and the email would contain a link to their site. ALLOWED_HOSTS makes Django refuse any host it doesn't recognise.\
How this differs from CORS:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
ALLOWED_HOSTS\'a0answers "what names can people use to reach\'a0this backend?" It lists the backend's own address.\
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS\'a0answers "which\'a0websites\'a0may call this backend from a browser?" It lists the frontend addresses, such as\'a0phuyenenglish.com\'a0and the Firebase site.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 That's why the frontend domains belong in the CORS list and not in ALLOWED_HOSTS.\
Locally the same check applies, which is why your .env has localhost,127.0.0.1,10.0.0.171: those are the names you use to reach the Django server on your Mac.\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
