\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 It is, and that's exactly why it belongs in ALLOWED_HOSTS: the list holds the backend's own address or addresses. The naming has three layers:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
django-no-redis\'a0is the Heroku\'a0app name, the label you use with the\'a0heroku\'a0command (-a django-no-redis).\
\ls2\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
django-no-redis-74168f6e866e.herokuapp.com\'a0is the\'a0domain Heroku gave that app: the app name, a random suffix, then\'a0.herokuapp.com. It's the web address your Django server is reached at, and the one in your frontend's\'a0VITE_API_URL.\
\ls2\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Django itself\'a0doesn't know which domain it's running under. Heroku forwards each request to the server process, and Django only sees the\'a0Host\'a0header the request arrived with.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 So ALLOWED_HOSTS is how you tell Django its own name: "I'm reached at django-no-redis-74168f6e866e.herokuapp.com, so only accept requests addressed to that." The frontend sites in the list were never names for this server, which is why they were redundant.\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
