\cf2 \cb3 So the whole ALLOWED_HOSTS  thing is about Django, not Heroku.\
12:14 PM\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Yes. ALLOWED_HOSTS is a Django setting, and Django is what checks it. You'd need it on any host (Render, AWS, your own server), and you need it locally too, which is why your .env has localhost,127.0.0.1,10.0.0.171.\
Heroku has two smaller roles:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It gives you the domain,\'a0django-no-redis-74168f6e866e.herokuapp.com, which is the name you list.\
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It stores the value\'a0as a config var instead of a\'a0.env\'a0file, the same way it stores your other settings.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Heroku itself never reads ALLOWED_HOSTS and doesn't care what's in it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
