\cf2 \cb3 can the same thing be said about CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGIN? Is it only about Django, too?\
Thought for 1s\
12:19 PM\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Mostly. The setting CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS (plural) is purely on your backend, and Heroku plays the same two small roles as before. The differences are in who uses the setting and who enforces CORS:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The setting belongs to an add-on package.\'a0It comes from\'a0django-cors-headers\'a0(the\'a0corsheaders\'a0entry in\'a0INSTALLED_APPS), not core Django. Django on its own knows nothing about CORS.\
\ls4\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The browser enforces CORS.\'a0This is the main difference from\'a0ALLOWED_HOSTS, where Django rejects the request itself. With CORS, Django mostly just\'a0answers\'a0the request and adds a header like\'a0Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://animated-joy-489014-v1.web.app\'a0when the calling site is on the list. The\'a0browser\'a0then reads that header and decides whether the frontend's JavaScript may see the response.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa51\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 That's why your error appeared in the browser console: the Firebase origin wasn't in the list, so Django sent no such header, and the browser blocked the response.\
It also means CORS only protects users in a browser. A script or a tool like curl ignores those headers completely and can call your API regardless. Authentication and CSRF protect your data; CORS only stops other websites from using a visitor's browser to read your API.\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
